<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">Boyle Abbey</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">founded <a href="/years/1161">1161</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Boyle Abbey is a ruined Cistercian monastery in Boyle, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/county-roscommon">County Roscommon</a>, Ireland, founded in 1161 as a daughter house of Mellifont. Its church, completed in the early 13th century, combines Romanesque and Gothic features. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/macdermot">MacDermots</a> of Moylurg were its patrons; it was suppressed in the 16th century and later used as a military barracks.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/boyle-abbey.jpg" alt="Boyle Abbey" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Andreas F. Borchert · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Boyle_Abbey_Nave_1997_09_17.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Saint Malachy brought the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cistercians">Cistercians</a> to Ireland to reform its church, and their first abbey was founded at Mellifont in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/county-louth">County Louth</a> in 1142. In 1148 Peter O'Mordha and twelve companions were sent to Connaught to found a daughter house in Moylurg. They tried Grellachdinach, Buniffi and Drumcunny before a local chieftain, McGreevy, gave them the old monastery of Assylin, near a ford where roads from four directions met. Finding that site too high above the river, the monks built a few kilometres to the east, where running water could serve their kitchen, mills and fish ponds.</p><p>Granted some 50,000 acres in 27 granges west of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/river-shannon">River Shannon</a>, the house founded Knockmoy Abbey in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/county-galway">County Galway</a> and Assaroe Abbey in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/county-donegal">County Donegal</a>. In 1202 it was occupied and badly damaged in a war over the kingship of Connaught, and its buildings were finished only around 1218, when it was consecrated. Boyle took part in the Conspiracy of Mellifont of the 1220s, an attempt by Mellifont's daughter houses to break from Norman control, and was attacked in 1235 and 1284. Its abbots were often made <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishops</a> of Elphin, and by the end of the 15th century the family of the local chieftains had taken control of it.</p><p>After its suppression under Queen Elizabeth the abbey's lands passed in 1569 to Patrick Cusack of Gerrardston, though the monks were at first allowed to stay. The last abbot, Gelasius O'Cullenan, was executed in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dublin">Dublin</a> in 1580, and the site was leased to William Ussher in 1589 and to Sir John King in 1603.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>The monastery followed the usual Cistercian plan: a church on the north side of a roughly rectangular cloister, with the chapter house, the kitchen and refectory, and probably storehouses with a dormitory above on the other sides. Little of the cloister survives, as the Elizabethans made it a barracks in 1592 and the Cromwellians, who besieged it in 1645, did so again. The church has an aisled nave, transepts with a pair of chapels each, a chancel and a squat square tower added over the crossing in the 13th century.</p><p>Its design follows models from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burgundy">Burgundy</a>, while the cylindrical piers of the nave's south arcade echo the West of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>. The carved corbels and capitals, probably the work of local masons of the School of the West, are among the most inventive architectural sculpture of the early 13th century in the west of Ireland. The abbey is now a national monument in state care.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Boyle Abbey” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The MacDermots ruled from the Rock of Lough Ce, an island fortress on Lough Key, and patronised the abbey of <b>Boyle</b> and the learned families who compiled the Annals of Loch Ce.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-moylurg">Kingdom of Moylurg</a></li></ul></section>
